## CP01 — Maxwell algebra, conservation and two gauge cautions (mathematics)

Q is symmetric because F_acg^cdF_bd is symmetric in a,b. Its trace is F²−4F²/4=0. At a normal tetrad its energy and mixed components are T(n,n)=(|e|²+|b|²)/(2μ₀), T(n,e_i)=−(e×b)_i/μ₀. For the spatial contraction use Σ_kε_ikℓε_jkm=δ_ijδ_ℓm−δ_imδ_ℓj and the negative temporal contraction; thus these formulas carry the −+++ signs explicitly. In SI the mixed component is −S_i/c, S=E×B/μ₀.

For Maxwell fields with smooth current j, ∇^aT_ab=−F_bcj^c. Indeed product differentiation gives μ₀^−1[(∇^aF_ac)F_b{}^c+F_ac∇^aF_b{}^c−¼∂_bF²]. The first term is −F_bcj^c by the stated Maxwell sign. Contract ∇_aF_bc+∇_bF_ca+∇_cF_ab=0 with F^ac. The first and third contracted terms coincide after renaming and skewness, while the middle is −½∂_bF². Hence F^ac∇_aF_bc=¼∂_bF², canceling the remaining bracket. This is tensorial and valid on any smooth metric; metric compatibility justifies moving indices. Source-free stress conservation follows. Its flat analogue is EM C5, whose entire actual calculation was inspected; no flat-to-curved derivative commutation is assumed.

If F=dA locally, ∇^aF_ab=□_gA_b−∇_b(∇^aA_a)−Ric_b{}^cA_c. Expand ∇^a(∇_aA_b−∇_bA_a) and use the covector curvature commutator from GR G3 to obtain the displayed Ricci term. Thus Lorenz gauge ∇^aA_a=0 gives □A_b−Ric_b{}^cA_c=−μ₀j_b, not an uncoupled collection of scalar metric waves. Ricci involves second derivatives of g; substituting unreduced Einstein equations into this term before gauge propagation can conceal circularity. CP04 instead uses F itself and no potential wave reduction.

On a coordinate star-shaped ball with dF=0, A_a(x)=∫_0¹t x^bF_ba(tx)dt has dA=F: differentiate each component, use ∂_kF_ba−∂_aF_bk=∂_bF_ka from closure, and identify the integrand of ∂_kA_a−∂_aA_k as d/dt[t²F_ka(tx)]. FTC gives F_ka. Gauge A→A+dχ preserves F by commuting scalar partial derivatives. On a smooth metric, imposing Lorenz gauge locally is possible by solving the scalar linear wave □χ=−∇·A with specified smooth Cauchy data; GR E0/E1's wave reduction has positive time matrix and arbitrary smooth forcing, so it gives an actual local solution. This potential statement is local. A closed two-form with nonzero integral over a closed two-cycle is not globally dA, since Stokes would make that integral zero. Field-strength evolution covers such sectors without adding a fictitious global potential.

