## CP03 — Explicit field-strength symmetric reduction and propagation (mathematics)

In coordinates (s,x^i) with spacelike s levels, write g=−α²ds²+h_ij(dx^i+β^ids)(dx^j+β^jds), α>0 and h positive. Put n=α^−1(∂_s−β^i∂_i); choose spatial orthonormal e_i by three-step Gram–Schmidt on the coordinate fields. Its denominators stay positive on the selected metric chart. The tetrad coefficients are smooth algebraic functions of g; their derivatives are smooth functions of (g,g^−1,∂g), introducing no second metric derivatives.

Evolve the six components (e,b) by i_n dF=0 and i_n d*F=0. In this tetrad the leading derivative equations are n(e)−curl_eb=lower order and n(b)+curl_ee=lower order. To verify them, freeze the tetrad at a point and substitute F_0i=−e_i,F_ij=ε_ijkb_k: the (0,i,j) closedness equations are b_s+curl e=0, and the spatial ∇_aF^{ai}=0 equations are e_s−curl b=0. Derivatives of tetrad components and the volume/Hodge coefficients are precisely the remaining first-derivative metric terms. At the point, noncommuting tetrad brackets act algebraically on F and are also such lower-order terms. These component equations, together with the two divergence constraints, exhaust the four independent equations in each of dF=0,d*F=0.

For a spatial covector ξ define C(ξ)v=ξ×v, so C(ξ)^T=−C(ξ) and ||C(ξ)||≤|ξ|. The six-dimensional principal matrix for a covector ζ is A_M(ζ)=ζ(n)I₆+[[0,−C(ζ_sp)],[C(ζ_sp),0]]. It is real symmetric. Its second block has operator norm at most |ζ_sp|, so it is positive if ζ(n)>|ζ_sp|, exactly a future-positive timelike covector. In the slice-adapted frame e_i(s)=0, the coordinate time matrix is A_M(ds)=α^−1I₆, uniformly positive on a compact admissible metric set; coordinate spatial matrices are symmetric combinations of the same displayed blocks and −β^iα^−1I₆. This verifies symmetric hyperbolicity rather than only a real characteristic polynomial. The characteristic eigenvalues of the spatial block are 0,0,+|ξ|,+|ξ|,−|ξ|,−|ξ|, from C²v=ξ(ξ·v)−|ξ|²v; physical transverse propagation is null, while the longitudinal constraints are transported by the chosen reduction.

Constraint propagation is an independent complete argument for any resulting metric. Set U=dF and V=d*F. The evolution equations imply i_nU=i_nV=0, so each is a spatial three-form: U=q i_nvol_g and V=r i_nvol_g for unique scalars q,r. This follows by expanding the four 3-form components in an adapted tetrad. dU=dV=0 by d²=0, whereas d(q i_nvol_g)=div(qn)vol_g by GR G4's direct volume identity. Thus n(q)+q div n=0 and n(r)+r div n=0. The initial divergence constraints are q=r=0. Along each n integral curve, the linear scalar ODE has solution q=q₀exp(−∫div n), and similarly r; local flow existence is GR G2. Hence q=r=0 throughout a small development. This proof requires no Einstein equations, stress conservation or EM potential gauge condition, so it introduces no circular subsidiary dependence.

