## CP04 — Local coupled high-Sobolev/smooth theorem and Einstein subsidiary (mathematics)

For the constrained T³ data of CP02, s≥5, there is T>0 and a coupled electrovacuum solution with g∈L∞H^(s+1)∩CH^s, ∂_sg∈L∞H^s∩CH^(s−1), F∈L∞H^s∩CH^(s−1). The metric has Lorentz signature and spacelike s levels; all equations are classical by the embeddings at these orders. Smooth data yield a smooth solution on the same base-order interval. The gauge-reduced state is unique and continuously dependent in CH^(s−1) on the corresponding first-order data with uniform H^s bounds and fixed compact admissible metric neighborhood. No endpoint threshold or strong CH^s stability for the first-order state is asserted.

Proof: impose the reduced Einstein equation Ric_ab−∇_(aH_b)=κT_EM,ab+Λg_ab (tracefree T_EM). GR E1's verified expansion has principal term −½g^cd bar∇_cbar∇_d g_ab and remaining terms smooth in g,g^−1,bar∇g and prescribed smooth bar connection/curvature. Introduce U=(g,v=bar∇₀g,w_i=bar∇_ig,e,b). In the initially orthogonal chart, g^00<0 and g^ij positive. The first-order wave block is exactly GR E1's symmetric block with positive time weight diag(I,−g^00I,g^ijI); restrict to a compact small-shift metric neighborhood where these inequalities remain uniform. Maxwell adds CP03's independent positive time block and symmetric spatial blocks. Einstein source μ₀^−1Q uses only (g,e,b); Maxwell lower-order terms use only (g,v,w,e,b). Therefore there are no off-diagonal derivative couplings. All coefficients are smooth functions on this finite-dimensional open state domain, with smooth prescribed bar background, and the initial U lies in H^s with values in a compact admissible neighborhood. All of E0's actual hypotheses hold.

For clarity E0 is a proved construction, not just a named PDE theorem: its finite Fourier approximation uses the positive mass matrix P_NA⁰(U_N) on the truncated coefficient space, with inverse norm bounded by the least eigenvalue. Differentiating its mass equation first controls ∂_sU_N in H^(s−1); weighted H^s energy then uses symmetry to remove top spatial derivatives and the integer product/chain estimates to bound commutators. This gives a common lifespan with bounded norms and admissible coefficient values. Equicontinuity of finite modes and the H^s→H^(s−1) Fourier-tail estimate give strong CH^(s−1) convergence; passage against smooth Fourier tests gives the reduced equation. Weighted difference energy gives uniqueness and stability, and higher-order induction gives common-interval smooth persistence. These exact steps were fully inspected in E0, including its correction of the source's erroneous composition and weak-convergence shortcuts.

The derivative-definition constraints v=bar∇₀g,w_i=bar∇_ig hold initially. The wave reduction includes g_s=v plus the background connection conversion and w_i,s=bar∇_iv plus the known background commutator. Subtracting bar∇_ig from w_i gives a homogeneous linear equation in those differences (fixed background curvature terms cancel by the same commutator). Zero initial differences persist. Therefore the evolved w_i are actual metric derivatives. Their H^s bounds imply g∈H^(s+1): in periodic coefficient form, Σ_i||∂_ig||_s²+||g||_s² is equivalent to ||g||_(s+1)². Their CH^(s−1) continuity similarly gives g∈CH^s. Time regularity follows from its equation. The metric stays Lorentzian and the field reconstruction agrees with CP02's initial two-form.

CP03 now establishes the full Maxwell equations, independently of Einstein's H constraint; CP01 then gives ∇^aT_EM,ab=0. GR G3's actual signed contracted Bianchi proof and E1's residual calculation yield □_gH_b+Ric_baH^a=0. Indeed the unreduced Einstein residual is ½(∇_aH_b+∇_bH_a−g_ab∇_cH^c), whose divergence is half that wave expression after commuting the covector derivatives. H=0 initially implies all tangential derivatives zero. With g₀₀=−1,g₀i=0, its 00 and 0i residual components are ½∂₀H₀ and ½∂₀H_i. The CP02 Gauss/Codazzi constraints force both to vanish, hence initial normal derivative H_s=0. A linear variable-metric wave reduction has the same positive energy block, and its zero-data weighted energy satisfies E'≤CE. Thus H=0, and the reduced solution is an actual Einstein–Maxwell solution. At s≥5 all identities have sufficient classical derivatives; they can equally be passed from smooth approximation via the stated product bounds. No conservation of an arbitrary assigned T is assumed.

Smooth continuation in the chosen compact torus gauge holds while the first-order H^s norm stays uniformly finite and its state values stay in a fixed compact subset of the admissible coefficient domain: restart E0 near a proposed finite endpoint with its uniform bounded-set lifespan and use uniqueness on overlaps. Loss of gauge coordinate admissibility is distinct from a proved geometric singularity. This criterion asserts no all-time bound or completeness.

