## CP05 — Local geometric uniqueness, localization and maximal scope (mathematics)

For smooth constrained data on any Σ of CP00, the preceding reduction gives a smooth local geometric development unique up to the stated isometry near the initial embedding. Smooth geometric uniqueness includes the two-form itself, with no gauge choice for a nonexistent global potential.

On each relatively compact coordinate ball, choose a smooth reference background and extend the first-order data to a periodic coefficient domain, maintaining positive h and Lorentz admissibility on a collar; the extension need not satisfy constraints outside that ball. Such extension is concrete: shrink to a chart and a compact interior ball, keep the data on it, interpolate with a fixed constant admissible state in a slightly larger ball using a smooth cutoff. Positivity of spatial h is convex; initial orthogonal metric and first derivatives are freely extendible. For nonsmall metric coordinate images cover the compact by smaller state neighborhoods before extending. Zero Maxwell and Einstein residuals persist inside the ball's finite-speed development independently of the exterior extension.

The relevant finite-speed uniqueness is GR E2's inspected positive-energy cone argument. For a difference satisfying A⁰w_s+A^iw_i=Cw with bounded symmetric matrices and A⁰≥aI, choose a shrinking coordinate ball with speed L>a^−1Σ||A^i||. Its side flux in ∂_μ(wᵀA^μw) is nonnegative outward; integration and Gronwall force zero difference from zero base data. This gives independence of extensions on a small interior cone. For Maxwell, CP03's timelike-conormal positivity gives light-cone propagation; Einstein wave principal has the same causal cone. Smooth coordinate patches agree after a **common harmonic-map gauge**, not by assuming componentwise covariance of gauge-reduced equations.

Precisely, for two actual developments of the same data solve the harmonic-map wave equation to the same smooth background, with initial identity map and prescribed normal derivative. The equation is g^ab(∂_a∂_bΨ^A−Γ(g)^c_ab∂_cΨ^A+barΓ^A_BC(Ψ)∂_aΨ^B∂_bΨ^C)=0. Its first-order reduction has the positive wave block, with smooth coefficients on each given smooth development. E0 supplies a solution; the initial differential is invertible, so the local inverse theorem makes Ψ a diffeomorphism after shrinking. Pulling g and F into this gauge gives identical CP02 initial metric data and identical Maxwell data. CP04 gauge uniqueness makes them identical on an overlap neighborhood. The comparison Ψ₂^−1∘Ψ₁ is thus the desired isometry, fixes the initial surface and preserves time orientation. Normal/tangential derivative agreement prevents extra initial gauge freedom. This argument is smooth; high-Sobolev uniqueness claimed above is in the selected gauge, and no optimal rough geometric-uniqueness threshold is hidden.

A locally finite countable atlas and compatible uniqueness patch neighborhoods with possibly point-dependent times. GR H0–H2's actual causal neighborhood argument permits restriction to the domain of dependence of the initial embedded Σ, where it is Cauchy. Compact Σ allows a common positive time by a finite subcover; arbitrary noncompact data do not. H3/H4, fully inspected as research proofs with exact external source chains, allow the usual maximal globally hyperbolic development statement **only after** the source-free local existence and geometric uniqueness premises established here. The source proof is a global gluing/universal-embedding theorem, not a global smooth-time existence estimate. CP08 below gives the exact smooth source-free maximal-development theorem after current full source-proof reading; it makes no claim of geodesic completeness, unrestricted stability, cosmic censorship or regularity through singularities.

