## CP08 — Source-free smooth maximal globally hyperbolic development (mathematics)

**Exact claim.** For smooth constrained source-free data (Σ,h,K,e,b) with connected Σ as CP00, there is a smooth connected time-oriented globally hyperbolic Einstein–Maxwell development (M,g,F,i) into which every other smooth globally hyperbolic development of these data embeds by a time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding fixing i and carrying F. It is unique up to such isometry. Its class is smooth GHDs with the specified F sector; neither completeness nor maximality among arbitrary non-GHD/rough extensions is asserted. AC is the mathematical selection premise. Λ is any fixed constant.

**Exact causal supplier interface.** The complete inspected GR H0–H3 research arguments provide convex normal neighborhoods, geodesic/normal-chart uniqueness, causal limit curves and closed causal relation on GHDs, compact diamonds and compact J^−(q)∩J^+(Σ), continuous finite time separation with maximizing causal geodesics, small globally hyperbolic neighborhoods of spacelike hypersurfaces, smooth timelike flows/Cauchy splitting. Those arguments specify orientation/causal-curve regularity and the Riemannian auxiliary metric/compactness assumptions; none is a physical premise. The current external source is Sbierski arXiv:1309.7591, smooth conventions and matter extension on p11, Theorems2.6–2.8 p12, and the complete proofs §§3.1–3.3 pp12–24, all actually read here. GR H4's full source comparison was also read. Its needed local theorem2.6 is supplied for the actual (g,F) model by CP04/CP05, not by the source's vacuum statement. The following records all proof uses and the two completeness-of-chain repairs, rather than treating the source theorem as an unexplained citation.

1. **Unique identifications and maximal common domain.** If two isometric immersions agree in value and derivative at a point, exponential-map/geodesic uniqueness makes them agree on a normal neighborhood; their agreement set is closed by continuity and open by repeating that argument. On connected domains they agree everywhere. Isometries fixing Σ have equal differentials because tangent vectors and the future unit normal agree. Every connected component of an intersection of common GHDs meets the common Σ through a causal curve component, so the same argument gives consistent identification there. The additional F identifications agree because each map carries F. A time-oriented isometric immersion of GHDs taking one Cauchy surface diffeomorphically to the other is injective: timelike geodesics through two alleged preimages of a point have the same projected tangent, each meets Σ once, the projected geodesic meets the target Cauchy surface once, so the two crossings coincide and geodesic uniqueness gives the original points equal. This is source Lemmas3.1–3.3, fully checked. Therefore the union, **inside one fixed development**, of all common GHDs gives a maximal common development U and a compatible injective isometry ψ:U→M'. It is Cauchy: the component of an inextendible causal curve in a member meets Σ, and a second meeting is impossible in the ambient GHD. This is source Theorem3.4 with F carried along, not an arbitrary union of unrelated spaces.

2. **Corresponding-boundary argument.** A corresponding pair p∈∂U,p'∈∂ψ(U) means every two neighborhoods have related points in U. Source Proposition3.7 uses openness of chronology and closedness of J to prove that any timelike curve in U ending at p has image ending at p'. Source Lemma3.8 extends ψ smoothly near the pair by ψhat=exp_(q')∘dψ_q∘exp_q^−1 with q,q' interior points on that timelike curve. Metric and F agree with their pullbacks at the boundary by smooth continuity, including the derivatives needed for induced K. The set C of corresponding points is open in ∂U. If C is nonempty to the future, source Lemma3.9 follows a past null generator of the achronal boundary until a first point p whose causal past intersects that boundary only at p. If a generator left C while still on the boundary, its image null geodesic and compact/closed causal limits would give a corresponding endpoint, a contradiction. If no first exposed point were reached, the past-inextendible generator would miss the Cauchy surface. A broken subsequent null path would be timelike by the local push-up lemma and violate the common-domain Cauchy property. These steps are source pp15–18 and use exactly GR H0–H2's proved causal prerequisites.

3. **Restart across a spacelike piece.** Source Lemma3.11 uses compact J^−(q)∩J^+(Σ) and closed J to find a nearby future q for which the uncovered causal past is confined to a small compact causally convex neighborhood W of p. Continuous time separation τ_q attains a positive maximum τ₀ on its compact uncovered part. A maximizing radial normal geodesic could be extended backward if a maximum were attained in the uncovered interior, so maximum points lie on ∂U. The level τ_q=τ₀ inside W is a smooth spacelike hyperboloid piece S by the inverse exponential formula τ_q(r)=sqrt(−g_q(exp_q^−1r,exp_q^−1r)) and Gauss lemma. It is contained in U∪C and meets ∂U. Its induced h,K,e,b agree under the extended ψ because both smooth fields agree up to the boundary. CP05 local geometric uniqueness on these new constrained data extends the identification across S. The union with U is a strictly larger common Cauchy development (every new causal curve meets S and then U), contradicting maximal commonality. Thus the MCGHD has no corresponding boundary pairs. This is the full source Theorem3.6 argument pp18–20; the matter-field values and local CP05 premise are explicitly included.

4. **Pairwise Hausdorff gluing.** Glue M and M' along U using ψ. Quotient maps are open injections: the preimage of the image of an open set A⊂M is A in M and ψ(A∩U) in M'. A nonseparable pair would have to lie on opposite common boundaries and be a forbidden corresponding pair. Hence the quotient is Hausdorff and its two countable atlases give a smooth second-countable space. g,F and time orientations patch because ψ preserves them. A causal curve's component in one old development meets Σ; a second crossing would produce a curve component in U missing its Cauchy surface. The quotient is thus a common GHD extension. This checks source Theorem2.7 pp20–22 with the actual field F included, not only the metric.

5. **Set-sized representatives.** Every GHD has a smooth future timelike field (partition into local future timelike fields on its second-countable smooth manifold). Its maximal flow from Σ gives a diffeomorphism from an open neighborhood D⊂Σ×R onto M: each maximal flow line meets Σ once, so it is bijective, and its derivative is invertible since the transported spatial tangent basis plus flow vector remain independent. Flow smoothness and the local inverse theorem prove the assertion, source pp23–24. Thus representatives can be encoded by open D, graphs of g∈Sym²T*D and F∈Λ²T*D, time orientation and canonical initial embedding inside fixed bundles over Σ×R. Power set and separation make their admissible tuples a **set**. This explicitly carries the two-form into the source's set encoding; it does not choose representatives from a proper class of isometry classes.

6. **Universal quotient and second countability.** Glue this set along its unique maximal common identifications. Transitivity holds because intersection/composition of two common identifications gives a common Cauchy development of the first and third representatives, hence is contained in their maximal common domain. Each representative embeds openly and injectively. Pairwise common Hausdorff quotients separate any two inequivalent points; finite-covering of a compact causal curve segment reduces any alleged repeated Σ crossing to the pairwise contradiction. Fields and time orientation patch locally. The unrestricted quotient has not yet inherited a countable atlas merely from an uncountable collection: source p23 cites an external Geroch result here, so we use GR H4's explicitly proved replacement. Choose a countable dense set on Σ. At recursively chosen countably many points choose tangent bases and add endpoints of all rational timelike initial vectors for which the exponential is defined, forward and backward. Smooth dependence and finite timelike-geodesic approximation of a curve from Σ to any point show this countable union is dense in the quotient. Such connecting curves exist because Σ is Cauchy and every point lies on an extendible-to-inextendible timelike curve; this uses the proved Cauchy property, not completeness. At those selected points take all rational tangent balls on which the exponential is injective and nonsingular. They cover the quotient: place any point and a selected nearby point in a convex normal neighborhood and choose a rational ball containing the relevant inverse exponential vector. These countably many Euclidean charts have countable rational bases, hence the quotient is second countable. All selections are allowed by AC. This closes the global atlas prerequisite rather than merely importing an unread Geroch citation.

7. **Universal property and uniqueness.** Every GHD is isometric, with its two-form, to a chosen representative by step5, and therefore embeds in the quotient. This is the asserted universal extension property. Two such universal developments embed into each other fixing Σ; the composed identifications agree with the identity by step1, hence each embedding is onto and gives the required isometry. The quotient is a smooth globally hyperbolic Einstein–Maxwell solution because those are local equations and its Cauchy property was proved. This completes the theorem. It contains no all-time a priori estimate and permits singular/incomplete endpoints inside the class boundary.

